your vegetables, and you cannot have dessert." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379149} {:answer/id #uuid "9ca58dd3-81a2-5e75-bf06-3b71fe5517d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Either you eat your vegetables, or you cannot have dessert." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379150} {:answer/id #uuid "c603d67b-0a2a-57b8-a62f-31a8b835af08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Either you don't eat your vegetables, or you can have dessert." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379151} {:answer/id #uuid "0cb9d333-1346-5b47-a1f0-9297d9005f6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Don't eat your vegetables, or you cannot have dessert." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379152} {:answer/id #uuid "e7e7f4d1-92b5-5b76-9a70-b6dda138dcc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Either you eat your vegetables, or you can have dessert." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c603d67b-0a2a-57b8-a62f-31a8b835af08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Either you don't eat your vegetables, or you can have dessert." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379151} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1bf1056-ffc3-5ba7-bc23-3b671a26a339" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e854e89-958c-5853-be27-7985def8fc91" :question/math-academy-id "q-182458" :question/problem "$\\,$ If you eat your vegetables, then you can have dessert.\n\nWhich statement is logically equivalent to the conditional statement above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You eat your vegetables.\n\n- $B:$ You can have dessert.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A⇒B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A∨B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You don't eat your vegetables.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ You don't eat your vegetables, or you can have dessert.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ Either you don't eat your vegetables, or you can have dessert.\n\n**Note**: \n\nUsing the word \"Either\" helps clarify that the sentence structure presents two alternatives and is particularly useful when employing the disjunctive form $(¬P∨Q)$ in conversational or formal English. So, the phrase effectively conveys that one of the two stated conditions will hold true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060143 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059689 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d56b033-a2c8-57c7-adde-e574d726ec78" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting Symbolic Conditional Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1c916b6-4cf8-5907-89d3-31936135cf81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057225} {:answer/id #uuid "c0612680-3bd0-5412-af09-c14bae27086e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057226} {:answer/id #uuid "f1da4c9f-bd78-5b50-9956-065e949f3776" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057227} {:answer/id #uuid "182c9d29-ca2e-59ff-b0a0-d10f153e43f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057228} {:answer/id #uuid "673cf2ae-4e5d-5ef8-9d91-900651c436a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057229}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c69c688d-95aa-5518-a83c-0eb22c1c57f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186057224} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef745713-2d35-5b7c-8ffa-849019bc9a44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379162} {:answer/id #uuid "09ee9ee7-3301-505d-b0b4-fa2550135d7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379163} {:answer/id #uuid "e18cecf1-2078-5bce-b07e-ae6fcd9da67e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379164} {:answer/id #uuid "4a4e643c-4c88-5ee5-a9cb-bc94557a292e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379165} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f93cbd-83f5-5a7a-b05a-db016463f018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e18cecf1-2078-5bce-b07e-ae6fcd9da67e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379164} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8f9c689-8577-52b8-aa74-632d353fd931" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "866aa1bc-982d-58ab-8e07-dd5148fa5235" :question/math-academy-id "q-182470" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P⇒Q≡¬P∨Q$\n- $¬(P⇒Q)≡P∧¬Q$\n- $¬(P⇒Q)≡P∨¬Q$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $P⇒Q$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬P∨Q.$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. From part I, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\nP⇒Q≡¬P∨Q\n$$\n\nBy negating this statement, we get\n\n$$\n¬(P⇒Q)≡¬(¬P∨Q).\n$$\n\nDistributing the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law, we have\n\n$$\n¬(¬P∨Q)≡¬(¬P)∧¬Q.\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negation, we reach\n\n$$\nP∧¬Q.\n$$\n\nSo, $¬(P⇒Q)$ is equivalent to $P∧¬Q.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409584 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059689 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d56b033-a2c8-57c7-adde-e574d726ec78" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting Symbolic Conditional Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "776196c9-cd05-5240-9195-0b0ce27cb444" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409574} {:answer/id #uuid "8c6f81fa-ef3e-50ce-8cd2-a95e102cf1da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409575} {:answer/id #uuid "9ebe90ec-2497-5de0-b5bd-ba3d0c79d56c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409576} {:answer/id #uuid "cff472a8-e087-557d-80e6-5d535af2d5c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409578} {:answer/id #uuid "e1eca4c9-2b1c-5f16-824c-d7eea7cfd316" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409580}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "776196c9-cd05-5240-9195-0b0ce27cb444" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409574} :answer-field/id #uuid "7645b3d9-5bcf-5720-89a4-adc2f8073a1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1308109d-5efe-51ad-bfa1-4050522344dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-182506" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A⇒¬B≡¬(A∧B)$\n- $¬(A⇒¬B)≡A∧B$\n- $¬(A⇒¬B)≡¬A∧B$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A⇒¬B$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬A∨¬B,$ which by De Morgan's law is equivalent to $¬(A∧B).$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. From part I, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\nA⇒¬B≡¬(A∧B)\n$$\n\nBy negating this statement, we get\n\n$$\n¬(A⇒¬B)≡¬(¬(A∧B)).\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negation, we reach\n\n$$\nA∧B.\n$$\n\nSo, $¬(A⇒¬B)$ is equivalent to $A∧B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401729 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059687 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b60a8ae-c04f-530a-b89c-f8ac70969d0f" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Conditional Statements as Disjunctions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34a0563d-13f2-5256-b18a-47d8b5f64041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Buy a ticket, or you cannot enter the concert." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401731} {:answer/id #uuid "f6731b6b-f0cf-55d7-87cc-7c66343c06ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Buy a ticket, and you cannot enter the concert." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401732} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b9de9c-dfe1-5e38-920b-ffe3d2c1e6b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Don't buy a ticket, or you can enter the concert." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401733} {:answer/id #uuid "62a4e17c-f6e3-5d49-ae77-c163f6da3e78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Don't buy a ticket, and you cannot enter the concert." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401734} {:answer/id #uuid "20b3068d-8214-5c62-ae9b-55f280ed1b92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Buy a ticket, or you can enter the concert." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34a0563d-13f2-5256-b18a-47d8b5f64041" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Buy a ticket, or you cannot enter the concert." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401731} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f6ea998-1510-5ac2-a229-6d71f403c27f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cc2aafac-bd5b-5b06-b1c9-744a025e7988" :question/math-academy-id "q-182570" :question/problem "$\\,$ If you don't buy a ticket, then you cannot enter the concert.\n\nWhich statement is logically equivalent to the conditional statement above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ You don't buy a ticket.\n\n- $B:$ You cannot enter the concert.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A⇒B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A∨B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You buy a ticket.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ You buy a ticket, or you cannot enter the concert.\n\nWe can write this in more everyday language as follows:\n\n$\\,$ Buy a ticket, or you cannot enter the concert."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379260 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059688 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0936217d-66d2-5360-b88b-a30683720415" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Disjunctive Statements as Conditionals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d443744-cec7-50fa-b09c-8602760601c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you read the book, then you won't understand the lecture." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379262} {:answer/id #uuid "1c2fe104-86f6-5a71-b4ce-e3438ccc1c0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you don't read the book, then you will understand the lecture." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379263} {:answer/id #uuid "805f4b30-a877-5ba5-9626-70177aa117fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you don't read the book, then you won't understand the lecture." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379264} {:answer/id #uuid "f134f018-7f21-5ef0-9130-5829fb236386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Read the book, and you will understand the lecture." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379265} {:answer/id #uuid "a7cc6093-b2cb-518c-aaf2-d26b6ffa88bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you read the book, then you will understand the lecture." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "805f4b30-a877-5ba5-9626-70177aa117fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you don't read the book, then you won't understand the lecture." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379264} :answer-field/id #uuid "0274425a-3516-5cf1-ad83-3c6fa0c5f83d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "79056780-76f3-5ac1-9ec9-79ef55cc8595" :question/math-academy-id "q-182590" :question/problem "$\\,$ Read the book, or you won't understand the lecture.\n\nWhich conditional statement is logically equivalent to the disjunctive statement above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ Read the book.\n\n- $B:$ You won't understand the lecture.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A∨B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A⇒B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You don't read the book.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ If you don't read the book, then you won't understand the lecture."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379267 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059688 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0936217d-66d2-5360-b88b-a30683720415" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Disjunctive Statements as Conditionals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e868b646-15fe-548f-a72f-8ee36770e921" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Refill the gas, and the car won't stop." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379269} {:answer/id #uuid "80f7cf9e-eb09-518f-9521-39a0d9202ac4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you refill the gas, then the car won't stop." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379270} {:answer/id #uuid "a978c03f-369f-5a80-92bd-57f628e184dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you don't refill the gas, then the car will stop." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379271} {:answer/id #uuid "885eb7dc-c6a9-526b-9770-abdefeec79e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you don't refill the gas, then the car won't stop." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379272} {:answer/id #uuid "655b6822-2da8-54fd-98c3-85d75068c1e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you refill the gas, then the car will stop." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379273}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a978c03f-369f-5a80-92bd-57f628e184dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "If you don't refill the gas, then the car will stop." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379271} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c2ba26c-4972-5aeb-95e6-6fdc0e9051b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379268}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c932ba97-f4d8-5039-85c1-76029cd575f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-182593" :question/problem "$\\,$ Refill the gas, or the car will stop.\n\nWhich conditional statement is logically equivalent to the disjunctive statement above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote by $A$ and $B$ the following statements:\n\n- $A:$ Refill the gas.\n\n- $B:$ The car will stop.\n\nThen, our initial statement can be written as $A∨B,$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬A⇒B.\n$$\n\nThe negation of $A$ can be written as\n\n- $¬A:$ You don't refill the gas.\n\nTherefore, the given statement is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ If you don't refill the gas, then the car will stop."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409586 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eec6cf11-74cc-5b3a-b2fe-bd4285fe4a88" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting Conditional Statement Using the Associative, Commutative, and Absorption Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "191c421c-bbe9-5286-9beb-03027543bcc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B∨¬C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409566} {:answer/id #uuid "33e9615f-fd80-5202-949f-ebfef4f3e8b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬B∨C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409569} {:answer/id #uuid "35dc661b-8042-5dd9-ae2c-1ddb0493316b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B∧¬C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409570} {:answer/id #uuid "442d26c2-04f3-57d7-adf7-53465469c631" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬B∧C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409572} {:answer/id #uuid "4c2b2d55-0e4e-5769-9706-03ec9ecc763c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B∨C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "191c421c-bbe9-5286-9beb-03027543bcc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B∨¬C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409566} :answer-field/id #uuid "eea492f1-156d-5834-96c0-8454fc274282" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c7953127-b916-5d8d-98b4-eeece8a2e1ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-81892" :question/problem "Simplify the statement $(¬B∧C)⇒(B∨¬C).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that\n\n$$\n(¬B∧C)⇒(B∨¬C)≡¬(¬B∧C)∨(B∨¬C).\n$$\n\nWe can distribute the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law:\n\n$$\n¬(¬B∧C)∨(B∨¬C)≡(¬(¬B)∨¬C)∨(B∨¬C)\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negations, we reach\n\n$$\n(¬(¬B)∨¬C)∨(B∨¬C)≡(B∨¬C)∨(B∨¬C).\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the disjunction of a statement with itself:\n\n$$\n(B∨¬C)∨(B∨¬C)≡B∨¬C\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $B∨¬C.$"}]}
